You can avoid the cast by using a spread in `update`:
```
return {
  ...previous,
  spec: {
    ...previous.spec,
    cronExpressions: [],
    intervals: [{ every: 60_000, offset: 0 }]
  }
}
```
You're hitting Typescript's [excess property checks](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/objects.html#excess-property-checks). The types for `update` are technically correct, but assignment to `previous.spec` adding a `cronExpression` isn't valid since `previous.spec: ScheduleSpecDescription` which doesn't have `cronSchedule`, but you can return an object with `cronSchedule` as that is valid on the `update` callback return type.

I'm hesitant to add this to `ScheduleSpecDescription` as this field won't ever be present and there isn't a good way to indicate this to users.
